diff --git a/lab/hints.md b/lab/hints.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..9fbf9d87f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lab/hints.md
@@ -0,0 +1,182 @@
+# Lab Hints
+
+Based on upstream commit `8f1a4e3316e4d3a6743bb35abee57122776ddcc1`.
+
+Short, practical notes for working in the lab.
+
+## Spin up the lab
+
+Three VMs, builder plus mgmt plus worker. Drive the cluster from builder, mgmt and
+worker only run their own playbook. `build.yml` builds the checkout it lives in and
+never clones, so put the repo on builder first.
+
+1. Provision the three VMs from `lab/heat/lab-stack.yaml`.
+2. builder: `docker login oci.s3rj1k.fyi`, then from the checkout
+   `ansible-playbook lab/ansible/build.yml -K`. Add `-e nico_clean=true` to reclaim
+   disk before a rebuild, or `-e image_tag=v0.0.1` to override the derived tag.
+3. mgmt: `ansible-playbook lab/ansible/mgmt.yaml -K`.
+4. worker: `ansible-playbook lab/ansible/worker.yaml -K`.
+5. builder: save mgmt `sudo k0s kubeconfig admin` to `~/.kube/config`.
+6. builder: `lab/patches/apply.sh`, then `lab/scripts/env.sh` and `direnv reload` (env.sh wires lab.env into `.local_envrc`).
+7. builder: `helm-prereqs/setup.sh --skip-dpf` (dpuless lab).
+8. builder: `lab/scripts/register.sh`, then `lab/scripts/provision-instance.sh`.
+
+## Reading the chassis serial off a BMC
+
+`SERIAL` in `lab.env` reaches `register.sh` as `--chassis-serial-number` and must
+match the BMC exactly, so update the `env.sh` default after a hardware swap. The
+System id is vendor specific, look it up rather than guessing:
+
+```bash
+export BMC_USERNAME=root BMC_PASSWORD=... BMC_IP=...
+
+curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" \
+  "https://${BMC_IP}$(curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" \
+    "https://${BMC_IP}/redfish/v1/Systems" | jq -r '.Members[0]."@odata.id"')" \
+  | jq -r .SerialNumber
+```
+
+## Discovering the MACs for register.sh
+
+`env.sh` sets `BMC_MAC` and `BOOT_MAC` and `register.sh` passes both. A wrong
+`BOOT_MAC` means Kea never answers the host and it hangs in discovery.
+
+```bash
+# BMC MAC. Take the row whose address matches $BMC_IP.
+curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" \
+  "https://${BMC_IP}/redfish/v1/Managers/iDRAC.Embedded.1/EthernetInterfaces?\$expand=*(\$levels=1)" \
+  | jq -r '.Members[] | "\(.Id)  \(.MACAddress)  \([.IPv4Addresses[]?.Address] | join(","))"'
+
+# Host ports. LinkUp shows what is cabled, but not which segment it lands on.
+curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" \
+  "https://${BMC_IP}/redfish/v1/Systems/System.Embedded.1/EthernetInterfaces?\$expand=*(\$levels=1)" \
+  | jq -r '.Members[] | "\(.Id)  \(.MACAddress)  link=\(.LinkStatus)  speed=\(.SpeedMbps)"'
+
+# Boot order. The PXE entry names the NIC that actually sends the DHCP request.
+curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" \
+  "https://${BMC_IP}/redfish/v1/Systems/System.Embedded.1/BootOptions?\$expand=*(\$levels=1)" \
+  | jq -r '.Members[] | "\(.Id)  \(.BootOptionEnabled)  \(.DisplayName)"'
+```
+
+A dual port card gives two LinkUp rows with consecutive MACs and Redfish cannot say
+which one is patched. Ground truth is the DHCP request once nico-dhcp is up:
+
+```bash
+kubectl -n nico-system logs -l app=nico-dhcp --tail=200 | grep -i discover
+```
+
+## Switching an iDRAC between network boot and disk
+
+iDRAC returns `PropertyNotWritable` for `Boot.BootSourceOverrideTarget` and
+`BootSourceOverrideEnabled`, so the standard Redfish boot override does not work
+at all. Use the OEM attribute instead, it applies on next boot with no BIOS job:
+
+```bash
+ATTRS="https://${BMC_IP}/redfish/v1/Managers/iDRAC.Embedded.1/Oem/Dell/DellAttributes/iDRAC.Embedded.1"
+
+# read
+curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" "$ATTRS" \
+  | jq '.Attributes | with_entries(select(.key | startswith("ServerBoot.1.")))'
+
+# network boot (BootOnce Enabled = one-shot, Disabled = until changed back)
+curl -skL -u "${BMC_USERNAME}:${BMC_PASSWORD}" -X PATCH -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
+  -d '{"Attributes":{"ServerBoot.1.FirstBootDevice":"PXE","ServerBoot.1.BootOnce":"Disabled"}}' \
+  "$ATTRS"
+```
+
+`FirstBootDevice` takes `Normal, PXE, HDD, BIOS, FDD, SD, F10, F11, UefiHttp`.
+`HDD` boots disk, `Normal` hands control back to the BIOS `BootOrder`.
+
+iDRAC has no working `UefiHttp` boot device, so the HTTP boot URL comes from DHCP
+option 67 (served by nico-dhcp) rather than being pinned on the BMC.
+
+## Running admin-cli
+
+Use `lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh`. It runs `nico-admin-cli` inside the `nico-api` pod
+with the pod's own SPIFFE identity over mTLS. Takes any admin-cli args:
+
+```bash
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh machine show            # all machines
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh machine show <id>       # one machine by id
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh machine show -f json    # JSON
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh vpc show
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh network-segment show
+```
+
+The repo's `dev/bin/admin-cli.sh` targets the old minikube/forge-system layout
+(hardcoded `--context minikube`, `carbide-api`), so it does not work in this lab.
+
+## Machine validation runs no tests
+
+`Completed(Success)` on a validation run does not mean tests passed, it means
+nothing failed. Check the results, not the run state:
+
+```bash
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh machine-validation runs show
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh machine-validation results show --validation-id <id>
+```
+
+An empty table is normal here. No `container_auth` row in
+`machine_validation_external_config` means no registry auth for scout and no test
+images to pull. Add one with `machine-validation external-config add-update`.
+
+The only symptom is this line, which is harmless.
+`get_container_auth_config` traces it and returns `Ok(())`, as does the image pull.
+
+```
+level=ERROR msg="database error conversion" error="machine_validation_external_config not found: container_auth"
+```
+
+Validation still spends ~30 min on boot config repair and BIOS jobs, real work.
+
+## Two host NICs on one segment break instance allocate
+
+`instance allocate` fails with `found unknown segments` when a machine has two
+`Data` interfaces on one segment. Nothing is missing, the segment lookup returns
+each segment once so two interfaces count 1 against 2 (`validate` in
+`crates/api-db/src/instance_address.rs`). Happens whenever both uplink ports are
+cabled, and returns on every re-registration.
+
+```bash
+lab/scripts/admin-cli.sh machine-interfaces show
+```
+
+No admin-cli command drops the extra one. `machine-interfaces delete` refuses an
+interface with a machine attached and has no force flag, leaving
+`machine force-delete` and a full re-onboarding. To keep the machine Ready, delete
+the rows directly, address child first.
+
+```bash
+pgq() { kubectl exec -n postgres nico-pg-cluster-0 -c postgres -- psql -U postgres -d nico_system_nico -tAc "$1"; }
+pgq "BEGIN;
+     DELETE FROM machine_interface_addresses WHERE interface_id='<iface>';
+     DELETE FROM machine_interfaces WHERE id='<iface>';
+     COMMIT;"
+```
+
+`register.sh` does the same wholesale by truncating `machine_interfaces`. The row
+returns on the next DHCP from that port, so this unblocks one allocate but not the
+next register run. Durable fix is to stop that port PXE booting.
+
+## Ad-hoc grpcurl in the api pod
+
+For APIs the CLI does not expose yet. `register.sh` installs it through
+`ensure_grpcurl_pod`. By hand, the pod runs as root with a writable rootfs, so the
+static binary drops in. Ephemeral, goes on pod restart.
+
+```bash
+docker create --name g fullstorydev/grpcurl:v1.9.3 && docker cp g:/bin/grpcurl ./grpcurl && docker rm g
+POD=$(kubectl -n nico-system get pod -o name | sed 's|^pod/||' | grep '^nico-api-' | head -1)
+kubectl cp ./grpcurl nico-system/$POD:/usr/local/bin/grpcurl -c nico-api
+kubectl -n nico-system exec $POD -c nico-api -- chmod +x /usr/local/bin/grpcurl
+```
+
+Run it with the pod's own certs, the same identity `admin-cli.sh` uses.
+
+```bash
+kubectl -n nico-system exec -it deploy/nico-api -c nico-api -- \
+  grpcurl --cacert /var/run/secrets/forge-roots/ca.crt \
+          --cert  /var/run/secrets/spiffe.io/tls.crt \
+          --key   /var/run/secrets/spiffe.io/tls.key \
+          nico-api.nico-system.svc.cluster.local:1079 list
+```
